1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,575
Plastic Pollution in the Ocean.

2
00:00:02,525 --> 00:00:11,587
An estimated 8 to 12 million metric tonnes of plastic enter the ocean annually — equivalent to dumping a garbage truck's worth of plastic into the sea every minute.

3
00:00:11,587 --> 00:00:25,887
A 2015 study published in Science by Jambeck and colleagues calculated that coastal countries generated 275 million metric tonnes of plastic waste in 2010, of which 4.8 to 12.7 million tonnes reached the ocean.

4
00:00:25,887 --> 00:00:30,762
Without intervention, this figure could nearly double by 2025.

5
00:00:30,762 --> 00:00:41,437
The Great Pacific Garbage Patch, located between Hawaii and California, spans approximately 1.6 million square kilometres — roughly twice the size of Texas.

6
00:00:41,437 --> 00:00:58,262
A 2018 study in Scientific Reports led by Lebreton characterised this accumulation zone and found that 92% of its mass consists of larger objects, while 94% of the estimated 1.8 trillion pieces were microplastics (fragments under 5 millimetres).

7
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:06,400
Microplastics have been detected in every ocean basin and in marine species spanning all trophic levels, from zooplankton to whales.

8
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,625
The ecological consequences are severe.

9
00:01:09,625 --> 00:01:15,900
Marine animals ingest plastic debris, leading to internal injuries, starvation, and death.

10
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:26,912
Sea turtles mistake plastic bags for jellyfish; seabirds feed plastic fragments to their chicks; filter feeders such as mussels and oysters accumulate microplastics in their tissues.

11
00:01:26,912 --> 00:01:36,350
A 2022 study estimated that over 1,500 marine species have been documented ingesting plastic, a number that continues to rise.

